{"id": "EUVD-2026-76395", "enisaUuid": "e2db6e4f-0c87-3aef-acd3-b0a2e6db7b4c", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nopenrisc: fix arbitrary kernel memory access via or1k_atomic syscall\n\nsys_or1k_atomic() (syscall 244 in the \"or1k\" ABI) takes two user\npointers, v1 and v2, and swaps the words they point to in hand-written\nassembly.\n\n    l.lwz   r29,0(r4)\n    l.lwz   r27,0(r5)\n    l.sw    0(r4),r27\n    l.sw    0(r5),r29\n\nThe pointers are not checked with access_ok(). The four memory\naccesses also have no exception table entries.\n\nA caller passes a kernel address as either pointer, and the syscall\nreads from and writes to it directly.\n\nThis gives an unprivileged process a kernel read/write primitive. It\noverwrites kernel data such as the sys_call_table, gaining code\nexecution in kernel context.\n\nCheck both pointers before entering the critical section. Add fixups\nfor the four memory accesses so faults on valid but unmapped user\naddresses return -EFAULT.\n\n[shorne@gmail.com: fix comment style]", "datePublished": "2026-09-11T19:43:42", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-19T08:47:40", "baseScore": 7.8, "baseScoreVersion": "3.1", "baseScoreVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H", "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/bf310718c6fa6adeee06d207a55489546d860e2c", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/497cba0b02e5555d99fe9ee1dc478af743ab7f7a", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/1f2e92e499d863f81df1732af59b4a3559969193", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/574ae2ac2b314aa33498cd2c28add106cbe644f2", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a520e8cac54fb403f3800125b606f55fcad42cb9", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/d64a75369cd0f2ee79afcc9d9ca34a3890989379", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/b53435c079c78f89f70a62dd5a322cca4e292b34", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/78004e9a87f240df03e2f73120d291763c32e0a7"], "aliases": ["GHSA-9rpr-c8j9-pwgc", "CVE-2026-89489"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.14, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "1258b25e-9b36-329c-a1b7-d85495d7ff03", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.10.270"}, {"id": "471a8753-9078-36ca-884b-b8a728181719", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9d02a4283e9ce4e9ca11ff00615bdacdb0515a1a <a520e8cac54fb403f3800125b606f55fcad42cb9"}, {"id": "492613f3-d550-3fcf-977d-a5e65fa4fdc5", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9d02a4283e9ce4e9ca11ff00615bdacdb0515a1a <78004e9a87f240df03e2f73120d291763c32e0a7"}, {"id": "4c9393df-4039-315c-ae3d-ca378f2a2f05", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9d02a4283e9ce4e9ca11ff00615bdacdb0515a1a <b53435c079c78f89f70a62dd5a322cca4e292b34"}, {"id": "5cbd7bae-5e74-3557-978a-36dd77317844", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "3.1"}, {"id": "5d0552e7-fd2e-3cce-97f1-9ae28f40f317", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9d02a4283e9ce4e9ca11ff00615bdacdb0515a1a <bf310718c6fa6adeee06d207a55489546d860e2c"}, {"id": "6b43f4f2-5034-3cfe-a6b8-3515579c0e3c", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.157"}, {"id": "70f58be8-0661-3f9d-a8f1-2ee5d1e9e622", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9d02a4283e9ce4e9ca11ff00615bdacdb0515a1a <1f2e92e499d863f81df1732af59b4a3559969193"}, {"id": "87063801-a849-3be9-b876-dd54d32c0ecc", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "8d7dceca-9b54-3a09-8bc6-32626b5bf29d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.2.4"}, {"id": "996868fb-ae44-3ef2-b282-577985c8127a", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.15.221"}, {"id": "abef3659-2ebf-33f4-aa08-a13f03bdbecc", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"n